<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Pius IX</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1792">1792</a>–<a href="/years/1878">1878</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Giovanni Maria Mastai-Ferretti</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pope Pius IX reigned from 1846 to 1878, the longest pontificate in history. Driven briefly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> by the revolution of 1848, he lost most of the Papal States to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> in 1860 and Rome itself in 1870, after which he called himself a prisoner in the Vatican. He convened the First Vatican Council, which defined papal infallibility.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ix.jpg" alt="Pope Pius IX" width="320" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pius_IX,_by_Adolphe_Braun,_1875.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>No foreign government ever recognised the Confederacy: when Ambrose Dudley Mann reported a letter from <b>Pope Pius IX</b> addressed to &quot;the Illustrious and Honorable Jefferson Davis, President of the Confederate States of America&quot;, Secretary of State Judah P. Benjamin replied that Mann had mistranslated it and that it…</blockquote><a href="/empire/confederate-states-of-america">Confederate States of America · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pius IX</b> began as a reforming pope in 1846, but after the murder of his minister Pellegrino Rossi in 1848 he fled to Gaeta, and a Roman Republic was declared in 1849 until French troops restored him.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After a liberal regime in Italy ended the secular power of the popes, <b>Pope Pius IX</b> condemned liberalism in all its forms in the Syllabus of Errors of 1864, and in many countries liberals answered by expelling the Jesuits.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/liberalism">Liberalism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>In the bull Ineffabilis Deus, <b>Pope Pius IX</b> declared as dogma that the Virgin Mary was conceived without original sin.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-8">8 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After the murder of <b>Pope Pius IX's</b> minister Pellegrino Rossi in November 1848, the pope fled to Gaeta in the Kingdom of the Two Sicilies.</blockquote><a href="/state/roman-republic-1849">Roman Republic (1849)</a></li></ul></section>
